<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple Computer//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  THIS DELIBERATELY OVERWRITES THE openssh PACKAGE'S OWN PLIST, at the same
  path and under the same Label, so there is exactly one sshd job rather than
  two fighting over port 22. Our package is staged after the debcache merge
  (stageBlackb0xTree() calls stageDebcache() then stageBlackb0xPackage()), so
  the later write wins.

  WHY. On the first device boot where the system partition merge had fully
  landed, sshd did not come up. The package's plist is socket-activated:

      inetdCompatibility  Wait = false
      Sockets  Listeners  SockServiceName = "ssh"
      StandardErrorPath   /dev/null

  Three things wrong with that here, and the third is why the first two went
  unnoticed for so long.

    1. `inetdCompatibility` is a launchd 1.0 facility. This device runs the
       XPC-era launchd, where it is at best vestigial. A job that is never
       triggered never fails, so there is nothing in any log to find.
    2. `SockServiceName = "ssh"` is resolved through /etc/services. We ship no
       /etc/services and do not control what the stock tvOS image carries, so
       the port that job binds is not a thing this project can assert. A name
       lookup that fails takes the whole job with it.
    3. StandardErrorPath is /dev/null. Whatever sshd had to say about any of
       this was discarded before it was written. On a device whose only output
       channel took a week of work to obtain, throwing stderr away is the
       expensive part of the bug, not a detail.

  So: no socket activation, no name lookup, and both streams go to a real file
  on the system partition. sshd is started directly and kept alive, which is
  what the original blackb0x did too -- its disassembled launchd staged its own
  com.openssh.sshd.plist as a loose top-level file rather than relying on the
  package's (see misc/README.md's record of what the original installed).

  Program vs ProgramArguments[0] is not a mistake. launchd execs `Program` but
  passes `ProgramArguments` as argv, so the wrapper runs with $0 = /usr/sbin/sshd
  and $@ = -D. /usr/libexec/sshd-keygen-wrapper is a /bin/sh script that
  generates the three host keys into /etc/ssh if they are missing and then
  `exec /usr/sbin/sshd $@`. That matters twice over: no host keys ship (only
  moduli, ssh_config and sshd_config do), and /etc is /private/etc on the
  SYSTEM partition, so the keys it writes land on the volume that accepts
  writes rather than the one that refuses them. Expect the first start to take
  a moment while it generates them.

  `-D` rather than `-i`: -i is inetd mode, which expects an already-accepted
  connection on stdin and is meaningless without the socket activation above.
  -D is "daemon, but do not detach", which is what a launchd job wants -- a
  process that forks away would be reaped and restarted forever.

  UsePrivilegeSeparation is `no` in the shipped sshd_config, so this needs
  neither /var/empty nor anything else on the data partition. That is load
  bearing right now: /var is not populated until the first-boot postinstall
  daemon migrates the stage, and a job that needed /var/empty would fail on
  exactly the boot where someone is trying to SSH in to find out why.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
      <string>com.openssh.sshd</string>
    <key>Program</key>
      <string>/usr/libexec/sshd-keygen-wrapper</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
      <array>
        <string>/usr/sbin/sshd</string>
        <string>-D</string>
      </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
      <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
      <true/>
    <key>SessionCreate</key>
      <true/>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
      <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/sshd.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
      <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/sshd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
